Take StrictSubstr() offsets and sizes as uint64_t, so a 32-bit size_t cannot truncate them
before the bounds check.

ELF/Mach-O fields are 64-bit, but StrictSubstr(data, size_t off, size_t n) narrows them on
32-bit hosts (wasm32 here) before CheckedAdd() and the comparison with data.size(). A section
header with sh_size = 0x30 << 56 then passes as size 0. With e_shentsize = 0 and e_shnum = 0,
ElfFile::Initialize() takes the section count from that sh_size, every ReadSection(i) reads the
same in-bounds header, and the section loops run for ~2^62 iterations (FindSectionByName's
Elf64_Word index never reaches the count at all). 64-bit builds reject the file with
"region out-of-bounds"; bloaty on a 32-bit host and the wasm fuzz build hang on a 476-byte
file. Adds that file to tests/testdata/fuzz_corpus/ (named by its SHA-1 like the others), so
fuzz_test runs it: it passes on 64-bit hosts either way, and on a 32-bit host it hangs without
the fix and is rejected with it (checked with the wasm32 fuzz target before and after the fix).
Not fixed upstream as of ccb49b0, which is also HEAD on 2026-11-21.
Upstream: none found (no related google/bloaty issue or PR on 2026-11-20; PR #390 touches
StrictSubstr callers for a different problem).

